WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.479
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.150
BROWN: It's a pleasure
to welcome everyone

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:18.340
to MIT on this bright
and glorious morning.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:20.640
I'm Bob Brown, the
provost of MIT,

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.940
and I'm standing in for
President Charles Vest, who

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.500
happens to be traveling.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.040
He, Chuck, would very
much love to be here.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:30.720
During his time
as president, this

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:33.450
will be the only Nobel
laureate that we've

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:37.530
received that he does
not introduce personally.

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:40.950
It's a glorious day
for American science,

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:44.640
for MIT, and for the
Department of Physics

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.610
with the awarding of the
Nobel Prize in physics

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:50.250
to Professor Frank Wilczek.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:54.060
I'm going to let Marc Kastner,
the department head in physics,

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:56.000
make the formal introduction.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.020
Mark?

00:00:58.020 --> 00:00:59.460
KASTNER: Thanks, Bob.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:04.349
Before I begin, I'd like to
introduce two other physics

00:01:04.349 --> 00:01:06.630
Nobel laureates who are here--

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:10.716
Jerry Friedman and
Wolfgang Ketterle.

00:01:10.716 --> 00:01:14.118
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:27.200
Jerry shared the prize
with the late Henry

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:33.650
Kendall, another faculty member
in our department, in 1990.

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:38.510
And when I introduced Wolfgang
to the department colloquium

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:43.130
after his Nobel Prize
was announced in 2000,

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:45.800
I pointed out that we
had had five Nobel Prize

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:49.130
winners in the physics
department in 25 years.

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:51.850
And I thought that once every
five years was pretty good,

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:54.440
but the faculty should
really work a little harder.

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:56.990
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:56.990 --> 00:01:59.741
And here you see
they come through.

00:01:59.741 --> 00:02:01.154
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:12.200
Frank Wilczek is, as Bob
Jaffe says, a luminary.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:16.970
He is one of the great
minds of modern physics.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:23.750
In 1972, when Frank
began his research

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:26.120
on quantum
chromodynamics, the theory

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:30.410
of the strong interactions,
we knew there were quarks.

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:33.880
And we knew it because of the
experiments of Jerry Friedman

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:36.920
and Henry Kendall.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:40.850
And a lot was known,
but the pieces

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:42.740
of what held these
quarks together,

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:46.520
the strong interaction,
were fragmented.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:48.710
There was no way of
putting it together.

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:54.920
The work that Frank did with
David Gross and Politzer

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:57.710
put things together in a
way which allowed physics

00:02:57.710 --> 00:02:59.030
to move forward.

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:01.760
And it is one of the
great cornerstones

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:04.960
of our understanding
of modern physics.

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:09.270
Frank came here from
Princeton about the time

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:10.600
of the millennium.

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:15.370
And it really began a new
century for MIT physics.

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:18.624
It gives me great pleasure
to introduce him to you.

00:03:18.624 --> 00:03:22.082
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:36.111 --> 00:03:36.902
WILCZEK: Thank you.

00:03:36.902 --> 00:03:40.360
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:46.776 --> 00:03:47.276
Thank you.

00:03:47.276 --> 00:03:50.734
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:50.734 --> 00:03:51.722
All right.

00:03:51.722 --> 00:03:54.750
That's enough, that's enough.

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:56.660
OK.

00:03:56.660 --> 00:04:00.470
An occasion like this I think
is, first of all, an occasion

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:02.090
to give thanks.

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:06.890
And I have a lot of
people I want to thank.

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:12.470
I want to thank my
parents who were

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:14.330
first-generation Americans.

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:20.010
My grandparents emigrated
under difficult circumstances

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:22.580
around the time of World
War I. And my parents

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:25.190
grew up in very
difficult circumstances

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:27.620
in the Depression,
but worked very

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:31.520
hard to get themselves
educated and then

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:33.710
to support my education.

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:35.840
And I was very pleased
to be able to call them

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:38.610
this morning with this news.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:46.920
I also want to thank
the United States for--

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:48.150
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:51.900
--supplying the
system of education

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:53.370
which did so well by me.

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:56.390
I'm a public school
guy all the way

00:04:56.390 --> 00:05:02.280
and a beneficiary of the
excellent public schools

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:04.230
of New York City in the 1950s.

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:09.950
And I think it's very important
that the country continue--

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:16.280
or recover-- the excellence in
its education that it's had.

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:19.660
I also got excellent
instruction at Chicago

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:24.280
before coming to Princeton,
where after that it

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:25.398
gets famous.

00:05:25.398 --> 00:05:28.150
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:33.250
I also want to thank
my wife, Betsy.

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:36.970
It was the time I was
meeting and courting

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:39.010
Betsy that I did this
work, and I don't think

00:05:39.010 --> 00:05:40.570
that's entirely a coincidence.

00:05:40.570 --> 00:05:43.680
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:47.020
We're still together,
and she's here,

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:50.320
and I thank her very much.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:52.810
Although they didn't have
much to do with this work,

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:54.370
I'd also like to
thank my daughter

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:58.620
for making life a lot
of fun and enabling

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:03.400
me to keep happy and productive,
I think, at a reasonable level

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:05.950
ever since.

00:06:05.950 --> 00:06:08.260
More seriously,
I'd like to thank

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:10.720
the community of
physicists, I guess

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:14.020
represented by Jerry
Friedman here especially,

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:16.810
the community of
physicists who really

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:21.040
laid the foundation
of insight and facts

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:25.210
that we were able to leverage
into a fundamental theory

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:26.680
of the strong interaction.

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:30.722
And our input definitely
was important--

00:06:30.722 --> 00:06:32.430
I don't want to be
too modest about this.

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:35.040
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:39.320
--but it absolutely was
not from out of thin air.

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:43.750
It was rooted in hard
experimental work

00:06:43.750 --> 00:06:48.730
and theoretical insights from
a large number of communities.

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:51.340
We stood on the
shoulders of giants,

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:54.340
but also on the shoulders of
a lot of only reasonably tall

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:59.380
people, a very large number of
average height people, and even

00:06:59.380 --> 00:07:03.250
a few dwarfs were important.

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:07.360
Finally, I'd like
to thank nature

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:11.290
for being so kind
as to really become

00:07:11.290 --> 00:07:17.170
simple and understandable
at short distances.

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:21.220
Our work is really a
vindication of the whole idea

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:25.450
that it is possible to
understand nature precisely

00:07:25.450 --> 00:07:32.620
and mathematically by
studying the fundamentals that

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:34.720
go on at short distances
and high energies

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:37.930
and building up from there.

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:40.840
So I'd like to thank mother
nature for her good taste--

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:42.970
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:42.970 --> 00:07:50.020
--in choice of principles, and
symmetries, and rationality.

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:54.100
So I don't know what
I'm supposed to do now.

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:55.970
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:55.970 --> 00:07:58.660
So maybe I'll just
say a few words

00:07:58.660 --> 00:08:01.240
about what's up on the board,
which is representative,

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:04.900
and then take questions.

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:07.815
So starting over here--

00:08:07.815 --> 00:08:10.098
BROWN: Can you grab
the microphone right

00:08:10.098 --> 00:08:11.966
up front, the wireless?

00:08:11.966 --> 00:08:13.680
[INAUDIBLE]

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:14.430
WILCZEK: This one?

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:15.885
BROWN: No, no.

00:08:15.885 --> 00:08:17.825
WILCZEK: Oh, in here.

00:08:17.825 --> 00:08:21.220
Oh, that.

00:08:21.220 --> 00:08:21.750
OK.

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:22.249
So--

00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:26.540
BROWN: It's on.

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:34.799
WILCZEK: --this this was the
result of the calculation that

00:08:34.799 --> 00:08:38.250
came from calculating
processes in space and time

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:42.270
where gluons interact
with other gluons

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:48.480
and modify the distribution
of colored charge.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:51.360
Or they can also go through
intermediate quarks.

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:55.320
And there are a number of
different kinds of processes.

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:57.210
I won't go through
the details, nor--

00:08:57.210 --> 00:08:59.590
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:02.550
--give you a quiz on this.

00:09:02.550 --> 00:09:05.790
But the result of
this calculation

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:14.640
was a formula for
how the coupling

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:18.920
of the strong
interaction changes

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:20.960
as a function of energy.

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:27.170
And what we found is that as a
result of quantum fluctuations

00:09:27.170 --> 00:09:33.200
and rearrangements of
color fields in the vacuum,

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:34.660
in empty space--

00:09:34.660 --> 00:09:37.850
what you call the
vacuum, in empty space--

00:09:37.850 --> 00:09:43.550
the effect of a charge at
higher and higher energies--

00:09:43.550 --> 00:09:46.310
or equivalently, at shorter
and shorter distances--

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:49.080
gets weaker and weaker.

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:51.440
This is what's called
asymptotic freedom.

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:55.010
And because the its effect
gets weaker and weaker,

00:09:55.010 --> 00:09:57.920
the particles behave more
and more as if they were free

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:01.400
of any interactions, as the
energies get larger and larger

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:03.840
or the distances get
smaller and smaller.

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:05.960
That's why it's called freedom.

00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:08.240
It's also simplicity,
because it means

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:12.650
that the complications
due to the interactions

00:10:12.650 --> 00:10:17.450
become less and less severe or
less and less necessary to take

00:10:17.450 --> 00:10:21.050
into account as you go into
higher energies or shorter

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:23.480
distances.

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:28.070
So that was the formula from
pen and paper calculations.

00:10:28.070 --> 00:10:31.040
And the original calculations--

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:32.870
because they had
many cross-checks

00:10:32.870 --> 00:10:34.760
and they involved
techniques that

00:10:34.760 --> 00:10:38.210
were pioneering at the time--
really filled notebooks.

00:10:38.210 --> 00:10:43.790
Nowadays, that
calculation is often

00:10:43.790 --> 00:10:46.730
assigned as an exercise in
quantum field theory classes.

00:10:46.730 --> 00:10:49.460
It's still difficult to do
in less than a page or two,

00:10:49.460 --> 00:10:55.190
but that was the
core calculation.

00:10:55.190 --> 00:10:59.960
From that calculation it was
a relatively straightforward--

00:10:59.960 --> 00:11:01.400
but not trivial--

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:05.780
exercise to derive
experimental consequences.

00:11:05.780 --> 00:11:07.760
In a variety of
different experiments,

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:11.850
which I'll be happy to
explain if someone asks,

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:16.680
one can check whether
this predicted behavior

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:18.570
of the effective
charge getting weaker

00:11:18.570 --> 00:11:24.120
and weaker at high energies and
short distances in fact occurs.

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:28.770
And most gratifyingly--
well, this is, of course,

00:11:28.770 --> 00:11:32.310
entirely manufactured data, but
gives the spirit of the actual

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:34.740
curve, which I didn't bring in--

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:37.680
our prediction for how the
strength of the coupling

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:40.290
should vary as a
function of energy

00:11:40.290 --> 00:11:44.220
has been vindicated now by
many thousands of measurements

00:11:44.220 --> 00:11:47.340
and literally hundreds of
different kinds of experiments.

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:55.520
And so I still find it a miracle
to think that the calculations

00:11:55.520 --> 00:11:59.780
and concepts that on the one
hand you scribble on paper

00:11:59.780 --> 00:12:04.680
match the results that are
measured in accelerators with

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:08.610
these gigantic magnets and
detection apparatus' that seem

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:11.460
to come from an entirely
different universe of discourse

00:12:11.460 --> 00:12:12.370
and concepts.

00:12:12.370 --> 00:12:18.590
And yet, one maps onto the other
with this amazing accuracy.

00:12:18.590 --> 00:12:21.210
And then I'll display
one more thing.

00:12:21.210 --> 00:12:23.360
So this all has to
do with the behavior

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:25.190
of the so-called strong
interaction, which

00:12:25.190 --> 00:12:29.540
is responsible for building
up protons and neutrons out

00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:32.600
of quarks and for the
interactions of protons

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:36.440
and neutrons that are
responsible for building up

00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:38.960
atomic nuclei and
holding them together

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:41.900
and responsible for our mass.

00:12:41.900 --> 00:12:43.550
So if you think
you're overweight,

00:12:43.550 --> 00:12:44.540
you have me to blame--

00:12:44.540 --> 00:12:46.350
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:46.350 --> 00:12:46.850
--mostly.

00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:57.540
This one is one of
the theoretical uses

00:12:57.540 --> 00:13:00.090
of this insight.

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:01.800
We would like to
construct, as much as

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:04.320
possible, a unified description
of the different forces

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:05.910
of nature.

00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:07.560
But when we go out
and measure, we

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:10.740
find that several of
the different forces

00:13:10.740 --> 00:13:13.890
don't seem to want to be unified
in that they appear to have

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:15.690
very different strengths.

00:13:15.690 --> 00:13:22.230
So here, alpha-3 is the inverse
strong coupling constant--

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:25.490
alpha-S is the same as alpha-3--

00:13:25.490 --> 00:13:27.700
this coupling constant
that describes the strength

00:13:27.700 --> 00:13:29.080
of the strong interaction.

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:32.290
Here its inverse,
so it increases

00:13:32.290 --> 00:13:33.670
as the energy increases.

00:13:33.670 --> 00:13:36.280
And instead of getting a
curve, because I plotted it

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:39.730
with a logarithm, it gives
more or less a straight line.

00:13:39.730 --> 00:13:41.150
But the details don't matter.

00:13:41.150 --> 00:13:45.250
The point is that the
coupling constant changes

00:13:45.250 --> 00:13:47.320
as a function of the energy.

00:13:47.320 --> 00:13:49.900
And likewise, the
coupling constant

00:13:49.900 --> 00:13:52.570
for the weak interactions
and for the electromagnetic

00:13:52.570 --> 00:13:56.410
interactions change depending
on the energy or distance

00:13:56.410 --> 00:13:58.060
at which you measure them.

00:13:58.060 --> 00:14:01.610
And likewise, even the
gravitational interaction.

00:14:01.610 --> 00:14:04.670
And so you can see if
the dream of unification

00:14:04.670 --> 00:14:09.650
at short distances
can really be achieved

00:14:09.650 --> 00:14:13.250
by extending and extrapolating
this kind of calculation

00:14:13.250 --> 00:14:15.830
to other interactions
and to higher energies.

00:14:15.830 --> 00:14:22.830
And miracle of
miracles, it works--

00:14:22.830 --> 00:14:23.940
well, more or less.

00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:25.428
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:27.910 --> 00:14:31.090
It works quantitatively
in detail,

00:14:31.090 --> 00:14:34.000
doing justice to the
accuracy of the experiments.

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:37.630
I should say, before
getting carried away,

00:14:37.630 --> 00:14:42.140
but what's actually measured
is what's in this oval here.

00:14:42.140 --> 00:14:44.360
And the rest is extrapolation.

00:14:44.360 --> 00:14:46.940
And those of you who
know about logarithms

00:14:46.940 --> 00:14:48.980
will know that to
get a logarithm

00:14:48.980 --> 00:14:52.080
to change by a factor of 10 is
a very enormous extrapolation.

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:54.630
So that's the sort of thing
we're talking about here.

00:14:54.630 --> 00:14:58.250
We're going from a 100
GeV in energy to 10

00:14:58.250 --> 00:15:01.970
to the 18th GeV in energy to
make this extrapolation-- so,

00:15:01.970 --> 00:15:03.560
far beyond what we've measured.

00:15:03.560 --> 00:15:05.990
But because of the
success of the theory,

00:15:05.990 --> 00:15:10.770
we have some confidence
that it makes sense to do.

00:15:10.770 --> 00:15:13.710
And when you do
it in full detail,

00:15:13.710 --> 00:15:15.440
you find that it
more or less works

00:15:15.440 --> 00:15:18.420
with the particles we know.

00:15:18.420 --> 00:15:21.740
However, if you really wanted
to get it to work accurately,

00:15:21.740 --> 00:15:23.810
you'd have to up
the ante and include

00:15:23.810 --> 00:15:26.450
something that is called
low-energy supersymmetry.

00:15:26.450 --> 00:15:29.450
Now, low-energy
supersymmetry is something

00:15:29.450 --> 00:15:32.310
that has consequences.

00:15:32.310 --> 00:15:34.740
It's something
that has to show up

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:37.170
at the next great
accelerator, the Large Hadron

00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:38.610
Collider at CERN.

00:15:38.610 --> 00:15:40.860
So we're actually
making predictions

00:15:40.860 --> 00:15:42.450
for the near future.

00:15:42.450 --> 00:15:44.809
We can be shot down or not.

00:15:44.809 --> 00:15:46.600
I'm not going to give
back my Nobel Prize--

00:15:46.600 --> 00:15:49.946
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:51.860 --> 00:15:53.130
--if it doesn't work.

00:15:53.130 --> 00:15:56.510
On the other hand, I'll be in
the market for a second one--

00:15:56.510 --> 00:15:59.807
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:59.807 --> 00:16:01.691
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:01.691 --> 00:16:10.630
But this is an example of how we
build on insights from the past

00:16:10.630 --> 00:16:12.550
to makes predictions
for the future.

00:16:12.550 --> 00:16:17.280
And it's a very exciting future
indeed for this kind of work.

00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:19.600
So with that, I'll
take questions.

00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:23.380
KASTNER: Before questions,
let me take this opportunity

00:16:23.380 --> 00:16:26.830
to introduce one more of
our department's Nobel Prize

00:16:26.830 --> 00:16:28.720
winners, Sam Ting.

00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:31.961
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:34.280 --> 00:16:37.220
Sam was actually our
first physics Nobel

00:16:37.220 --> 00:16:40.940
Prize at MIT in 1976.

00:16:40.940 --> 00:16:44.990
And all the ones
before this year

00:16:44.990 --> 00:16:47.270
were in experimental
physics, so this

00:16:47.270 --> 00:16:48.720
is a breakthrough year for us.

00:16:48.720 --> 00:16:50.840
This is our first prize
in theoretical physics.

00:16:55.290 --> 00:16:56.937
BROWN: Be glad to
take questions.

00:17:03.905 --> 00:17:05.530
MODERATOR: [INAUDIBLE]
phone [? mob. ?]

00:17:05.530 --> 00:17:08.720
Ask the phone if they
have any questions.

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:11.499
BROWN: People on the phone,
do you have any questions?

00:17:11.499 --> 00:17:12.879
REPORTER: Hi, yes.

00:17:12.879 --> 00:17:14.259
My name is [INAUDIBLE].

00:17:14.259 --> 00:17:15.546
I'm from the Washington Post.

00:17:15.546 --> 00:17:16.170
WILCZEK: Hello.

00:17:16.170 --> 00:17:20.270
REPORTER: I wonder if you could
describe your collaboration

00:17:20.270 --> 00:17:21.980
with your two
colleagues, where it took

00:17:21.980 --> 00:17:25.407
place and the circumstances.

00:17:25.407 --> 00:17:25.990
WILCZEK: Yeah.

00:17:25.990 --> 00:17:31.370
Well my collaboration was
with David Gross only.

00:17:31.370 --> 00:17:34.610
David Gross was an
assistant professor

00:17:34.610 --> 00:17:35.600
when we began the work.

00:17:35.600 --> 00:17:40.760
He got promoted to associate
professor somewhere in there.

00:17:40.760 --> 00:17:45.650
And he was my thesis advisor.

00:17:45.650 --> 00:17:48.215
And we worked very
intensely together.

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:56.910
David Politzer was a
graduate student at Harvard.

00:17:56.910 --> 00:17:59.870
And we learned of
his work really

00:17:59.870 --> 00:18:05.900
only after both groups had
found their main results.

00:18:05.900 --> 00:18:09.590
We learned of his work
through Sidney Coleman,

00:18:09.590 --> 00:18:15.025
who was a brilliant physicist,
David Politzer's thesis

00:18:15.025 --> 00:18:15.525
advisor.

00:18:18.710 --> 00:18:22.010
David Politzer was at Harvard
and Sidney was on leave

00:18:22.010 --> 00:18:23.740
from Harvard to Princeton.

00:18:23.740 --> 00:18:28.380
And Sidney realized that we
were working on related things.

00:18:28.380 --> 00:18:31.670
So we knew of each other's
existence at an early stage

00:18:31.670 --> 00:18:37.310
and remained in
friendly competition

00:18:37.310 --> 00:18:40.436
by having occasional
communications.

00:18:40.436 --> 00:18:43.962
AUDIENCE: What does it mean
for you to have won this prize?

00:18:43.962 --> 00:18:45.170
WILCZEK: Makes me very happy.

00:18:45.170 --> 00:18:48.020
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:48.020 --> 00:18:49.315
It also makes me relieved.

00:18:51.920 --> 00:18:56.990
I'd be lying if I said
it came as a shock.

00:18:56.990 --> 00:19:00.380
The theory I've
thought for a long time

00:19:00.380 --> 00:19:03.120
was very, very important.

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:08.820
The data in favor of it has been
clear for at least 20 years.

00:19:08.820 --> 00:19:14.240
And so every year at this
time for the past 20 years

00:19:14.240 --> 00:19:18.770
or so, I've had an unpleasant
week and a sleepless night.

00:19:18.770 --> 00:19:22.032
[LAUGHTER]

00:19:22.929 --> 00:19:24.720
I'm very pleased that
that's all over with.

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:28.136
[LAUGHTER]

00:19:28.136 --> 00:19:31.552
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:41.960
But more seriously, I think
it's very welcome recognition

00:19:41.960 --> 00:19:45.200
for an endeavor--

00:19:45.200 --> 00:19:48.740
understanding the fundamental
interactions of nature

00:19:48.740 --> 00:19:55.070
and understanding them in a
precise mathematical way--

00:19:55.070 --> 00:19:57.830
that is one of the crowning
glories of our culture.

00:19:57.830 --> 00:19:59.480
It's one of the
things that people

00:19:59.480 --> 00:20:03.800
will think about
1,000 years from now,

00:20:03.800 --> 00:20:07.460
if there are still people,
or their robotic descendants,

00:20:07.460 --> 00:20:08.070
or whatever.

00:20:08.070 --> 00:20:13.040
But it's one of the
real gems of our culture

00:20:13.040 --> 00:20:16.470
that you can understand
nature in this way,

00:20:16.470 --> 00:20:18.770
and then when you do you
find very beautiful things.

00:20:21.660 --> 00:20:29.960
And I hope to pay back in the
sense of using this recognition

00:20:29.960 --> 00:20:34.130
to feed some of it back
into the field as a whole

00:20:34.130 --> 00:20:36.830
and make sure that it keeps
supported and keeps vigorous.

00:20:40.950 --> 00:20:42.780
BROWN: Yes, in the back.

00:20:42.780 --> 00:20:46.210
AUDIENCE: I was wondering if
you could in layman's terms

00:20:46.210 --> 00:20:49.180
describe this theory of
the strong interaction,

00:20:49.180 --> 00:20:50.930
because you kept
referencing back to that.

00:20:50.930 --> 00:20:51.150
WILCZEK: Right.

00:20:51.150 --> 00:20:53.810
AUDIENCE: And I need to have
that building block for me to--

00:20:53.810 --> 00:20:58.890
WILCZEK: OK, so what is
the strong interaction.

00:20:58.890 --> 00:21:03.510
When people in the
1930s started to get

00:21:03.510 --> 00:21:05.970
a reasonably mature
understanding of what

00:21:05.970 --> 00:21:09.600
atomic nuclei
were, they realized

00:21:09.600 --> 00:21:13.290
that atomic nuclei can be
thought of as built up out

00:21:13.290 --> 00:21:17.490
of protons and neutrons, but
that the interaction that

00:21:17.490 --> 00:21:19.620
held them together between
protons and neutrons

00:21:19.620 --> 00:21:22.860
had to be a fundamentally
new interaction.

00:21:22.860 --> 00:21:25.650
The interactions that
were known at the time--

00:21:25.650 --> 00:21:28.080
electromagnetism and
gravity-- were not

00:21:28.080 --> 00:21:31.310
sufficient to hold
atomic nuclei together.

00:21:31.310 --> 00:21:33.680
So this became the problem
of the strong interaction--

00:21:33.680 --> 00:21:35.900
what is it that holds
atomic nuclei together.

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:40.560
That's not an easy
thing to investigate,

00:21:40.560 --> 00:21:42.135
because nuclei are
very, very small.

00:21:46.940 --> 00:21:52.800
And so the experiments were
at first relatively crude.

00:21:52.800 --> 00:21:57.270
They consisted in throwing
different particles together

00:21:57.270 --> 00:22:00.690
at high energies and
seeing what came out.

00:22:00.690 --> 00:22:03.960
And people hoped, I
suppose, that what came out

00:22:03.960 --> 00:22:09.290
would be simple building blocks
and that you would understand

00:22:09.290 --> 00:22:12.060
the protons and neutrons-- or
maybe the protons and neutrons

00:22:12.060 --> 00:22:16.600
themselves would be
deflected by a ways,

00:22:16.600 --> 00:22:21.370
then that would tell you
what their interactions were.

00:22:21.370 --> 00:22:26.470
But what happened instead was
that when the particles were

00:22:26.470 --> 00:22:31.630
smacked together you found
whole new worlds of phenomena,

00:22:31.630 --> 00:22:34.840
that there were many,
many unstable particles

00:22:34.840 --> 00:22:38.050
besides protons and neutrons.

00:22:38.050 --> 00:22:41.720
They have funny names like pi
mesons, rho mesons, lambda,

00:22:41.720 --> 00:22:44.090
[? sigma. ?] A lot of them
are just Greek letters.

00:22:44.090 --> 00:22:49.960
But anyway, though in a
way similar properties

00:22:49.960 --> 00:22:54.670
to protons and neutrons, they
had very powerful interactions.

00:22:54.670 --> 00:22:58.180
You could turn protons
into lambda particles,

00:22:58.180 --> 00:23:01.100
but then you could also turn
lambda particles into protons.

00:23:01.100 --> 00:23:06.000
So they seem to be more or
less on the same footing.

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:10.920
And understanding that
whole complex of phenomena

00:23:10.920 --> 00:23:14.760
became what's called the problem
of the strong interaction.

00:23:14.760 --> 00:23:17.590
And it got completely
out of hand by the 1960s.

00:23:17.590 --> 00:23:20.160
There were hundreds of
particles, no real rhyme

00:23:20.160 --> 00:23:24.480
or reason to why there were
so many or the patterns.

00:23:24.480 --> 00:23:28.410
Then primarily
Murray Gell-Mann--

00:23:28.410 --> 00:23:31.310
but with a lot of
help from others--

00:23:31.310 --> 00:23:33.780
started to see patterns
in this and found

00:23:33.780 --> 00:23:39.570
that he could understand the
observed kinds of particles

00:23:39.570 --> 00:23:42.750
by postulating that
they were made out

00:23:42.750 --> 00:23:45.900
of other things, quarks,
that had somewhat simpler

00:23:45.900 --> 00:23:47.560
properties.

00:23:47.560 --> 00:23:49.531
So some things were
made out of three quarks

00:23:49.531 --> 00:23:52.030
and other things were made out
of a quark and an antiquark--

00:23:52.030 --> 00:23:53.590
those are baryons and mesons.

00:23:53.590 --> 00:23:55.966
And those were the
strongly-interacting particles.

00:23:59.650 --> 00:24:02.440
There was no real understanding
of what a quark was other

00:24:02.440 --> 00:24:05.560
than it was some kind
of mysterious thing

00:24:05.560 --> 00:24:07.840
that you could build
other things out of.

00:24:07.840 --> 00:24:10.840
Part of the mystery was
that individual quarks

00:24:10.840 --> 00:24:13.820
were never discovered.

00:24:13.820 --> 00:24:15.920
They can't exist in isolation.

00:24:15.920 --> 00:24:22.760
And so this was clearly a
provisional understanding.

00:24:22.760 --> 00:24:28.060
Then, well, skipping a lot of
very interesting complications

00:24:28.060 --> 00:24:31.810
and very deep
insights, certainly one

00:24:31.810 --> 00:24:36.970
of the next great milestones
was when Friedman, Kendall,

00:24:36.970 --> 00:24:40.690
and Taylor, and
their collaborators

00:24:40.690 --> 00:24:48.100
did the very smart thing of
using a microscope to study

00:24:48.100 --> 00:24:50.137
inside of protons and neutrons.

00:24:50.137 --> 00:24:51.970
Of course this is not
the kind of microscope

00:24:51.970 --> 00:24:53.890
you buy at a hardware store.

00:24:53.890 --> 00:24:57.910
It's a microscope that
involves very, very high energy

00:24:57.910 --> 00:25:02.260
electrons and a lot of
interpretation of what you see.

00:25:02.260 --> 00:25:05.710
But using that, they were able
to probe inside the proton

00:25:05.710 --> 00:25:07.540
and show that--

00:25:07.540 --> 00:25:10.390
with some clever
interpretation-- what

00:25:10.390 --> 00:25:13.390
you were seeing was
something like quarks,

00:25:13.390 --> 00:25:16.150
and furthermore that the quarks
inside the proton at very

00:25:16.150 --> 00:25:19.540
short distances were interacting
with each other surprisingly

00:25:19.540 --> 00:25:22.000
weakly when they
got close together.

00:25:22.000 --> 00:25:24.630
So that posed the
problem for us--

00:25:24.630 --> 00:25:27.160
how can you get a
consistent description

00:25:27.160 --> 00:25:31.840
of entities, that is consistent
with the basic principles

00:25:31.840 --> 00:25:34.780
of quantum mechanics
and special relativity,

00:25:34.780 --> 00:25:37.420
that interact very
weakly at short distances

00:25:37.420 --> 00:25:39.370
but powerfully at
long distances,

00:25:39.370 --> 00:25:43.420
because we know quarks can't
separate from each other.

00:25:43.420 --> 00:25:48.190
And that at first seemed
to be a contradiction,

00:25:48.190 --> 00:25:54.520
because in most consistent
realizations of quantum

00:25:54.520 --> 00:25:57.820
mechanics and special
relativity charge tends

00:25:57.820 --> 00:26:01.610
to build up a long distances--

00:26:01.610 --> 00:26:04.470
I'm sorry, charge tends to get
shielded at long distances,

00:26:04.470 --> 00:26:07.580
so the effect of
coupling gets smaller

00:26:07.580 --> 00:26:10.700
at long distances, which is
the opposite of what we wanted.

00:26:14.410 --> 00:26:20.540
That's the ordinary behavior of
matter in dielectrics or things

00:26:20.540 --> 00:26:25.440
you learn about in ordinary
undergraduate physics.

00:26:25.440 --> 00:26:34.020
But what we found,
somewhat to our amazement--

00:26:34.020 --> 00:26:36.270
I think more to David's
amazement than to mine,

00:26:36.270 --> 00:26:39.439
because I didn't know as much--

00:26:39.439 --> 00:26:42.932
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:45.680 --> 00:26:50.440
--we found a unique
kind of theory,

00:26:50.440 --> 00:26:52.490
the so-called
non-abelian gauge theory,

00:26:52.490 --> 00:26:55.460
a theory with very remarkable
mathematical properties

00:26:55.460 --> 00:26:59.990
and high amounts of symmetry,
that had the opposite property,

00:26:59.990 --> 00:27:02.160
that instead of
charges canceling

00:27:02.160 --> 00:27:05.300
themselves and shielding
at large distances

00:27:05.300 --> 00:27:08.210
they anti-screened.

00:27:08.210 --> 00:27:10.310
So the charge would
grow at long distances,

00:27:10.310 --> 00:27:12.080
get smaller at small distances.

00:27:12.080 --> 00:27:16.040
So a very small seed
charge and weak interaction

00:27:16.040 --> 00:27:18.870
could build up to a
strong interaction.

00:27:18.870 --> 00:27:23.250
And those theories
were so rare, theories

00:27:23.250 --> 00:27:25.440
that had that kind of
behavior were so rare,

00:27:25.440 --> 00:27:28.200
that with only a few broad
hints from experiment

00:27:28.200 --> 00:27:29.820
and implementing
that requirement

00:27:29.820 --> 00:27:32.760
we were led to a unique theory
of what the strong interaction

00:27:32.760 --> 00:27:34.120
had to be.

00:27:34.120 --> 00:27:37.530
And that's what's now called
QCD, or Quantum Chromodynamics.

00:27:37.530 --> 00:27:40.710
So part of that was
introducing in addition

00:27:40.710 --> 00:27:44.340
to the quarks what are
called gluons, colored gluons

00:27:44.340 --> 00:27:46.030
with specific properties.

00:27:46.030 --> 00:27:49.620
So along the way we
discovered gluons

00:27:49.620 --> 00:27:52.320
and showed that the
interactions really

00:27:52.320 --> 00:27:55.160
simplified at short distances.

